# Entries created by a node which then leaves the network go missing on another
# node: #3 joins nw0, creates three entries, and leaves, and two of them are
# absent from one node at the end.
#
# That is the opposite order to entry-after-leave-rejoin.scenario, where the owner
# leaves and rejoins before creating. Both shapes turn on membership changing
# around a write, and it is not established whether they are one bug or two.
#
# Fixed by moby/moby#53437, which made bulk syncs carry network attachments. This
# failed one in thirteen at worst before that landed, and has since run 11,520
# consecutive executions without failing. The rates recorded below are from before
# the fix; they are what the attempts are sized against, so that a regression is
# caught rather than merely likely to be.
#
# moby/moby#53142 left every one of these rates untouched, so the credit is
# #53437's rather than the run of NetworkDB fixes in general.
#
# Found by TestNetworkDBAlwaysConverges on this tree at maxDelay=0: every action
# lands at one virtual instant, with no waits at all. The generator this was found
# with draws waiting per action against a per-scenario rate, so a scenario of pure
# burst is drawn about a tenth of the time; the previous generator, which drew
# waiting as one action in six, could not produce one at all.
#
# Its three networks are load-bearing. Dropping the actions of the two which do
# converge -- 35 of the 68 -- loses the failure, because their gossip still
# competes for memberlist's broadcast budget. Dropping the entries which converge
# is fine, and that is how it reduces.
#
# Measured before the fix, three times with -networkdb.convergence-measure, 12
# trials apiece, at about 1 in 9, 1 in 12 and 1 in 13, and 174ms an execution. The
# 60 attempts below are sized from the worst of those rather than the average: a
# budget is only worth stating if it holds on a bad day. At 1 in 13 they give about
# a 99% chance of catching it, for about 10.4s on a run which does not fail. That
# cost is from before the fix too; measured on the fixed tree this scenario runs in
# about 6.9s.
#
# This scenario is why the sizing is done that way. An earlier budget of 28, sized
# for 95% against the 1 in 9 measurement on its own, was seen passing with the bug
# present -- and against the 1 in 13 seen later, 28 is only about 89%.
scenario nodes=10 seed=0x2 attempts=60
         #2:JoinNetwork(nw2)
         #2:LeaveNetwork(nw2)
         #7:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #7:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
         #0:JoinNetwork(nw2)
         #0:LeaveNetwork(nw2)
         #3:JoinNetwork(nw0)
